WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.080
Good afternoon everyone, thank you for coming
to the Advanced Use of NCBI APIs webinar.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.120
Immediately pressing questions will be answered
by my colleague, Peter Cooper during in the

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.430
webinar.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.700
And more general questions will be answered
by me at the end of the webinar.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.520
Answers to these questions will be on our
webinars page in addition to questions we

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.060
may not get to.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.070
Also the slides are available at the URL that
you see at the bottom of this box.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:42.350
So, I know most of you are fairly technically
sophisticated, but at some point I think we

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.300
are all biologists.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:55.680
In that vein, what I want to do is to show
you tools and strategies, that I employ to

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:04.610
use NCBI API and FTP infrastructure, to answer
large-scale biological questions.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.039
Say I was interested in age-related molecular
degeneration.

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:15.710
A place I might start asking questions about
macular degeneration is ClinVar.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:21.469
In case you don't know, ClinVar is a crowd-sourced
database from the clinical genetics community

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:30.609
where they have asserted genotypes that are
associated with specific phenotypes such as

00:01:30.609 --> 00:01:32.130
age-related macular degeneration.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:40.840
If I were to use ClinVar and was interested
in one disease or one snp or a smal set of

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:47.179
them, I might in the search box and narrow
down by the filters on the left.

00:01:47.179 --> 00:01:53.899
However as a computational biologist, what
I find useful is to click on the FTP tab and

00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:57.710
go to the ClinVar FTP site.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:04.730
That will bring me to this site, and what
some people miss is that I can get vcf for

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:11.159
either GRCh38 or GRCh37 right now.

00:02:11.159 --> 00:02:19.700
This is a 30 MB download, and it has all of
the snps that are in clinvar.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:27.260
Most of the metadata that is on the website
is included in column 9 of the vcf and can

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.340
be filtered as such.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.549
The fields there should be reasonably obvious.

00:02:34.549 --> 00:02:42.141
A common use case for those involved in clinical
genetics, when you may have vcf file, for

00:02:42.141 --> 00:02:48.250
a given patient or a set of patients with
number of variants, sometimes variants are

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.610
expressed in HGVS notation.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:03:01.780
This can be ambiguous, so one thing I suggest
is that they use NCBI variation services API,

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.950
for disambiguating HGVS.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:09.359
If that is something you are interested in
please check out this URL, https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/variation/v0/

00:03:09.359 --> 00:03:16.930
and give this API a shot.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:24.870
ClinVar records can now be shown on a viewer
we have called iCn3D.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.939
This service is in alpha, but perhaps it would
be useful to you.

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:34.640
I encourage you to check it out, all you have
to do is re-create this URL,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.390
https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Structure/icn3d2/seq.html?id=809237
with the ID of the particular structure that

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.859
you are interested in viewing.

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:51.180
If it is in the PDB, you will be able to view
snps from clinvar on the structures.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:04:01.749
Finally, it is possible that your snps are
not phenotypically characterized.

00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:08.730
And in an event I will tell you about in a
bit, we built a tool called PhenVar, and that

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.769
tool allows for phenotypic characterization
of snps from dbSNP.

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:15.599
I want to demonstrate that to you now.

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:28.570
If I open up a web browser and I go to phenvar.colorado.edu,
what I can do, and I find this quite exciting,

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:43.880
is I can put in a particular snp, the rs number,
and generate some things I will show you as

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.380
well is doing some normalization.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:55.500
Here, I am doing a live search of PhenVar
and initially I get a word cloud of phenotypes,

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.190
phenotypic terms in fact.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:07.260
These are all of the phenotypic terms co-mentioned
with that snp in PubMed abstracts.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:13.340
Below I get something perhaps even more useful.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:25.130
This is a graph of the requested rs ID, linked
to articles, and then a view to other rs IDs

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.130
linked to those articles.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:32.260
If you are familiar with GWAS studies, you
are probably not terribly surprised by this

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:33.520
result.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:42.100
As you can see, there are a lot of snps associated
with diseases in particular studies that don't

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:48.720
map to others.However there are other snps
which seem to be fairly central to a number

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.460
of disease studies.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:58.680
I would like to demo PhenVar for a snp such
as this, in fact 800292.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:17.740
So if I search for 800292 under the same parameters,
I get a very similar word cloud, and amd is

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.070
even more central.

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:27.780
By the way this is Age-Related Macular Degeneration,
and I see several terms related to Age-Related

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.910
Macular Degeneration.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:37.410
One thing I see here is that I get a much
much denser graph, it is much denser of snps

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.880
and publications related to this thing.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:50.690
Here, I can see lots of publications that
are linked to this snp, 800292 and then other

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:58.510
publications that are a little more flung,
as we can see I have a much more dense network,

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:01.200
of phenotype-genotype associations.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:12.800
I would like to point out, this was built
using NCBI infrastructure.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:22.680
One of the pieces of infrastructure used to
build this is EDirect and also Eutils.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:29.600
One thing I find very useful is the EDirect
cookbook which we built about a year ago.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:38.320
We hypothesized that people might use commands,
simple commands directly from the Internet

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.730
to do relatively complex metadata investigation.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:48.400
We find this is a popular way to do this.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.461
Say you were interested in all genes aliases
associated with a particular disease in a

00:07:54.461 --> 00:08:01.070
particular organism, you could run a query
such as "liver cancer AND homo sapiens" and

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.150
find all of the relevant gene aliases.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:09.810
There are also many similar things you can
do and I encourage you to check it out.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:15.030
Towards the bottom of this list, there are
a bunch of resources for parsing literature

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.030
as well.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:23.760
I would like to think Mike Davidson as well
as Peter Cooper for working on this project.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.050
Finally.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:32.130
If there are Edirect queries that you would
like to know how to do, and you cannot figure

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:41.880
them out yourself, please put in issue in
github, at this repository and we will try

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:43.030
to help you out.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:50.740
I will tell you about some other tools we
use to do more complex things.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:58.340
In that sense a lot of these tools are built
on something that week call Magic-BLAST.

00:08:58.340 --> 00:09:07.600
This effectively allows you to edge computing
on NCBI's SRA database.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:14.910
The reason it allows you to do that is because
you can do affinities searches, in real-time

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.770
without moving the primary data sets.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.500
If you are interested in doing that.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:30.340
Please check out this URL, https://ncbi.github.io/magicblast
and think about downloading NCBI magic blast.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:35.980
The resources I am about to show you show
you, EDirect or Eutils, and magicblast are

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:42.590
typically pillars that we have used to stand
up these tools and may be useful when you

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:46.510
are generating your scripts for your tools.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:54.060
One thing we have been able to do is figure
out how to call SNPs on the fly.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:10:03.870
What we do in this pipeline is we use Eutils
to go into dbSNP, and find the flanking regions

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:14.170
for that snp, we then quickly create databases
from the regions of these SNPs, and we blast

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:19.990
SRA objects into them without doing any file
dumping.

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:22.740
This is streaming data.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:31.310
We further use that to create robots that
are able to go into remote SRA locations,

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:39.860
or even to remote fastq files, look for particular
sets of SNPs, and then anonymize them by random

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:41.710
flipping.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:50.880
Finally, we use this technology to integrate
phenotypes and genotypes estimating the phenotypic

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:59.900
relatedness by random forest models, and the
genotypic components by feedforward neural

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:00.900
networks.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:11.360
I would like to point out, this is all based
on magic blast and Eutils infrastructure for

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.130
being able to do these technically complicated
things.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:23.230
We are also able to do novel virus discovery
using these types of things.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:31.810
This is an early prototype we used to do novel
virus discovery, unfortunately it really required

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.200
doing some data dumping.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:41.870
Now we are able, by leveraging magic blast,
to do discovery without data dumping.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:49.570
One of the coolest parts of this is not only
can we assemble magic blast hits not only

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:57.400
by MEGAHIT, but Abyss, MetaSpades and assess
protein domains in them, we can create an

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:05.850
algorithm which is analogous to a De Bruijn
graph method where we actually extend the

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:16.320
contigs using magic blast streaming data from
SRA to detect whether there are domains of

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.770
other organisms around these viral contigs.

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:26.840
What that lets us do is to see if we have
novel endogenous viruses.

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:33.930
A lot of people contributed to this project
and Jan Buckman in my group is continusing

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:38.029
it wrapping this in Python currently.

00:12:38.029 --> 00:12:46.210
We are also able to use magic blast for antibiotic
resistance detection.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:55.779
This is a simple method, a very simple method
to detect antibiotic resistance genes in public

00:12:55.779 --> 00:13:00.279
genomic databases and datasets such as those
in the SRA.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:09.270
Effectively what it does, it removes host
genetic material using Magic-BLAST, and then

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:16.840
uses magic blast to map the CARD database
to the resulting read sets.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:21.010
It then produces a graph of these things.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:28.410
This was just published in the journal F100
Research and the program is called nasty bugs.

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:35.240
Finally, just last week we prototyped something
we call deSRA.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:45.540
what deSRA allows researchers to do is put
in individual gene IDs and a list of SRR accessions,

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:53.670
and through Magic-BLAST, we computed some
blast database, we were able to approximate

00:13:53.670 --> 00:14:00.990
expression for any genes in a particular SRA
data set.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:07.850
That is something that I think is exciting
because it gives an initial screening tool

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:13.610
to any raw, RNA-seq data set on the web.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:19.860
I would like to think the team that built
this, some of whom are listening to the webinar

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:21.100
right now.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:28.460
Why is Magic-BLAST able to stream things from
the SRA?

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:33.300
That has to do with the SRA toolkit infrastructure.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:41.260
Basically the toolkit allows for data streaming
from the SRA data sets.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:45.560
You can use these tools as they are right
now.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.330
They do many things.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:55.700
Here are some more complex ones, these are
simple ones.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:15:05.370
You can also integrate these into your code
and they have been integrated into both HISAT2

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:07.700
as well as GATK.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:15.210
So you can use those tools directly with NCBI,
SRA data sets.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:22.860
I highly recommend you check out some of these
URLs for further information.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:29.890
Finally a lot of these things were built in
NCBI Hackathons, or originally prototyped

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:32.240
in NCBI Hackathons.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:38.180
Here is a list of upcoming Hackathons, and
a live URL where you can find these.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:45.430
I will also add the publication for NastyBugs
very soon as well as some recent prototypes

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.000
we've worked on.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:52.380
Other projects were worked on or extended
in the NCBI visiting bioinformaticians program.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:16:00.360
If you are interested in coming to work at
NCBI for 4 to 6 weeks as an individual bioinformatician,

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:04.700
please email this alias for more information.

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:05.700
bioinformatics-training@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.020
Thank you very much.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.970
And with that I will take questions.

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:16.300
Somebody asked, is there a way to sign up
for a hackathon, it is not clear on the website?

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:21.960
On the screen I showed you, there were no
live links, but if you go to the site, as

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:29.200
it is now, some of the events have live links,
those will direct you to sign-up pages for

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:31.110
those events.

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:36.750
Please email this alias if you have any questions
about the hackathons.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:43.610
Somebody asked what experimental catalyst
is, the name has changed, but it is effectively

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:52.250
an alpha prototype for being able to document
both wet and dry bench lab work, in a visual,

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:54.530
Python framework.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:59.649
Someone else asked, they are interested in
meta data, is it possible to all metadata

00:16:59.649 --> 00:17:02.280
associated with a BioSample or BioProject?

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:05.600
For example the source of isolation for bacteria.

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:09.179
The answer is absolutely, and it's really
quite trivial.

00:17:09.179 --> 00:17:13.370
I highly recommend that you Google for the
EDirect cookbook.

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:21.449
There are specific examples on how to get
in to the metadata, the kind that you are

00:17:21.449 --> 00:17:27.189
interested in for biosamples and bioprojects
of different bacteria.

00:17:27.189 --> 00:17:34.489
What you can do is get the XML, for the whole
biosample record, there is an NCBI specific

00:17:34.489 --> 00:17:43.850
XML parser called xtract, that will parse
the metadata up from the XML for you.

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:49.860
So someone asked about the Magic-BLAST procedure.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:55.549
What I would say is, Magic-BLAST if you are
used to command line BLAST it is very similar

00:17:55.549 --> 00:17:57.820
with just a couple of caveats.

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:07.059
For one thing it is optimized for next generation
sequencing, that said, it will output by default

00:18:07.059 --> 00:18:11.990
to the SAM File, although you can get tabular
output as well.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:14.799
Just look at the help document.

00:18:14.799 --> 00:18:22.580
The binary can easily be downloaded from either
the NCBI BLAST Page, or from the github page

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:25.000
I showed you.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:30.710
Someone asks what languages most of these
tools are created in?

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:37.999
They are created in some combination of bash,
R, and Python.

00:18:37.999 --> 00:18:46.070
Someone else asked about analysis of snps
that cause alternative splicing, and gathering

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:51.519
information through the SRA Toolkit.

00:18:51.519 --> 00:18:57.610
That is a fairly difficult thing to do as
a whole, but one could envision building a

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.380
tool for that.

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:08.419
One thing you might be interested in doing
is taking RefSeq RNA, and building a blast

00:19:08.419 --> 00:19:12.299
database from your RefSeq RNA of interest.

00:19:12.299 --> 00:19:20.980
Then you can blast into the database, and
look for relative abundance of spliced isoforms

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:23.193
as well is snps of those in the RNA.

00:19:23.193 --> 00:19:29.360
That is something that you can do relatively
easily through Magic-BLAST.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:33.460
It is great to see there are a lot of questions.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:34.889
Are there any other questions?

00:19:34.889 --> 00:19:37.769
I will give it another minute or two.

00:19:37.769 --> 00:19:44.710
Actually, someone else said does Magic-BLAST
also work for assemblies?

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:53.731
I'm not entirely sure what you mean, but you
could use Magic-BLAST for whole genomes, it

00:19:53.731 --> 00:20:00.119
does work and it has very similar characteristics
to HISAT.

00:20:00.119 --> 00:20:07.049
Depending on what you are looking at, you
might want to try both Magic-BLAST and HISAT

00:20:07.049 --> 00:20:08.850
for transcript mapping.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:16.409
You can also use MagicBLAST in non-splice
mode to do DNA-seq mapping.

00:20:16.409 --> 00:20:21.470
Someone asked what tools we are using for
contig assembly?

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:28.649
I suspect you are talking about the novel
virus searching?

00:20:28.649 --> 00:20:37.100
Right now we are testing metaSPAdes, ABySS
2 as well as megaHIT.

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:43.289
If you have further suggestions, please feel
free to send bioinformatics-training an email.

00:20:43.289 --> 00:20:50.120
Also for specific applicaations, although
not so much for metagenomics, we are using

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:57.690
NCBI-built assembler called Skeesa, which
is freely available.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:06.039
If you are interested, I am giving a very
very short NCBI minute on Skeeza on November

00:21:06.039 --> 00:21:09.009
29.

00:21:09.009 --> 00:21:14.580
If you want to hear about some of the contigs
we've generated from bacterial pathogen data

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:19.690
sets. with SKEZA, please check that out.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:25.269
If you are interested in the Hackathons, we
are giving a workshop on running NCBI- NIH-style

00:21:25.269 --> 00:21:33.039
Hackathon's, I believe on December on December
10, but that is just off the top of my head;

00:21:33.039 --> 00:21:35.119
it is a Friday.

00:21:35.119 --> 00:21:42.129
Someone asked what Data Portal might be useful
to study population-based cancer genomics

00:21:42.129 --> 00:21:43.129
data?

00:21:43.129 --> 00:21:50.210
I'd encourage you to check out TCGA, which
is a large set of data put out by NCI, and

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:58.260
dbGaP also has quite a bit of cancer genomics
data, for some cancers, quite a bit more than

00:21:58.260 --> 00:21:59.330
TCGA.

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:11.080
We have provided a very short command line
tool to go between the TCGA and dbGaP data

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:14.149
set accessions for particular diseases.

00:22:14.149 --> 00:22:20.020
All right great with that I think we will
leave it here.

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:26.000
I would like to think everyone for attending
and I hope you have a fantastic rest of your

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:27.420
week.

